igin`\n- All passed the contentless scan first.\n- Fresh-clone recovery also passed: a clean clone fetched all three refs and exported **15,324 valid ev2 records**, with live matching the migration candidate and the ev1 archive present.\n\n## Manual stability test\n\nFrom a repo with substantial old history:\n\n```bash\nturnbridge resume codex\n```\n\nChoose a conversation that:\n\n- definitely predates this migration;\n- contains several turns and tool calls;\n- ideally originated in Claude Code or opencode, rather than Codex.\n\nVerify:\n\n1. It appears normally in the picker.\n2. The preview has a plausible source, turn count, and timestamps.\n3. Codex opens with the old conversation in context.\n4. Early and late portions are present—not truncated at the migration boundary.\n5. Tool calls/results remain paired and ordered.\n6. Actor/model/source attribution looks sensible.\n7. Send one harmless follow-up turn, then exit.\n8. Confirm the new turn was captured:\n\n```bash\ncledger log\n```\n\n9. Push normally and confirm there is no recursion, prolonged pause, or notes-ref rejection:\n\n```bash\ngit push\n```\n\nFor stronger confidence, repeat once with a second old conversation from a different source CLI. A multi-hop resume—resume the newly fabricated Codex conversation again—is useful, but that tests the turnbridge agent’s lineage work more than this migration.\n\n## When I’d call it stable\n\nAfter:\n\n- the fresh-clone smoke already passed;\n- one or two old conversations resume successfully;\n- new turns capture and push normally;\n- a few ordinary work sessions complete without scan, dedup, transport, or picker anomalies.\n\nThen make a cleanup PR removing:\n\n- `scripts/migrate-ev1-history.mjs`\n- the README migration section\n- `src/test/migration.test.ts`\n\nThe commits retain provenance. I’d keep:\n\n- `refs/notes/conversation-ledger-ev1` for a longer rollback window;\n- ev1 read tolerance for at least one additional stabilization cycle;\n- the local ma